  "summary": "In a regenerative turbine pump the liquid enters the side channel and is caught by the small vanes on the impeller rim, flung outward into the channel and returned to the vane roots further round — many times during one trip from suction to discharge. Each pass adds energy, so the pump develops far higher head than a centrifugal pump of the same size at small flows. It can handle some entrained vapour/gas, has a steep, almost linear H-Q curve and power rises toward shut-off, so it needs relief protection.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Rotodynamic (peripheral/side-channel)",
    "configuration": "Close-coupled, single stage, end-top ports"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "impeller": "36 radial vanes machined on a disc rim, floating axially between casing faces (typical)",
    "clearances": "Very small side clearances (hundredths of a mm range) — performance is sensitive to wear and solids"
  },
